---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { levels, pad, assistKind } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title="About" description="Who wrote these writeups, and exactly how AI was used." current="about">
  <header class="level-head">
    <div class="eyebrow"><span class="level-num">ABOUT</span></div>
    <h1>About this casebook, and how I used AI</h1>
    <p class="lede">I'm Elodie Ng, a psychology student. I entered TISC 2026 to see how far curiosity, research methods and decent tools could get someone who doesn't code for a living.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:32px">
    <h2 id="who">Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      I study psychology, and programming is not my strength. Several of these levels needed things I had never
      touched before, like reversing a Windows binary, decrypting Nintendo DS save data and racing a network service.
      What I did have was training in research methods. I know to run controls, to worry about reliability, and not to
      trust a result until the instrument has been checked. On Level 4 that mattered more than code.
    </p>
    <p class="profile-links">
      <a class="btn" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/elodie-ng/" rel="me noopener" target="_blank">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor"><path d="M4.98 3.5a2.5 2.5 0 1 1 0 5 2.5 2.5 0 0 1 0-5zM3 9.75h4v11H3zM9.5 9.75h3.8v1.5h.06c.53-1 1.83-2.06 3.77-2.06 4.03 0 4.77 2.65 4.77 6.1v5.46h-4v-4.84c0-1.15-.02-2.64-1.61-2.64-1.61 0-1.86 1.26-1.86 2.56v4.92h-4z" /></svg>
        Say hi on LinkedIn
      </a>
    </p>

    <h2 id="ai">How I used AI</h2>
    <p>
      I used AI throughout, and I'd rather say so plainly than let the polish of these pages suggest otherwise.
      Claude (in Claude Code) wrote and ran most of the scripts with me, and both Claude and ChatGPT helped me get up to speed on
      unfamiliar formats. The heavy model experiments ran on paid Google Colab GPUs, because my laptop runs models on its CPU at about two tokens a second.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Challenge</th><th>Split</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {levels.map((l) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={l.url}>L{pad(l.level)}</a></td>
              <td>{l.title}</td>
              <td><span class:list={['chip', assistKind(l)]}>{l.assist ?? (assistKind(l) === 'human' ? 'Human-led · AI-assisted tooling' : 'AI-assisted')}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>Levels 1 to 3</strong> were AI-assisted. The AI did much of the analysis, and I steered it, checked
      each step and wrote them up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Levels 4 to 7</strong> needed human calls the AI kept missing. On ZyGPT, the AI spent days treating the
      model's own description of its hidden payload as a trap, and it produced confident arguments that the flag
      wasn't there. The solve came when I read that description as an honest spec and ran the search myself. On
      Levels 5 and 6 I leaned on Claude and ChatGPT to learn the new ground quickly, and then decided what mattered. On Level 7 I
      had to stop the five-minute instance timer from rushing me, so I built the exploit locally before running it live.
    </p>

    <h2 id="misdirection">A CTF full of misdirection</h2>
    <p>
      TISC 2026 planted decoys everywhere. There were fake flags in binaries, strings written to tell an analyst (or
      an AI) to stop looking, a Ditto nicknamed "NOT ME!", and a chatbot that made up a new plausible flag every time
      you asked. By the end my rule was that nothing counts until the challenge itself confirms it, through a server
      response or a cryptographic check.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
